---
import PlaygroundShell from '../../components/PlaygroundShell.astro';
---
<PlaygroundShell title="Read a tablet" hint="A tablet keeps taking writes, and every full memtable becomes another file. Choose how many writes it has taken, whether compactions merge the files, whether Bloom filters are used, how big a block is, and whether the row you read exists. Watch how many files a read has to open and how many reads a server can do. A simple model with round numbers, not Google's code or data.">
  <ol class="how">
    <li>Press <b>Reset</b>. After <b>Few writes</b> a tablet has <b>5</b> files and nothing merges them. A read of a row that exists checks <b>3.0</b> of them on average, pulls <b>192.0</b> KB from the file system and a server can do about <b>391</b> random reads a second.</li>
    <li>Set <b>Writes so far</b> to <b>Many writes</b>. With nothing to merge them the tablet has <b>100</b> files, a read opens <b>50.5</b> of them on average, pulls <b>3,232.0</b> KB, and a server can manage only about <b>23</b> reads a second.</li>
    <li>Set <b>Compactions</b> to <b>Merge in the background</b>. The tablet is back to <b>5</b> files, a read opens <b>3.0</b> and the rate is back up to about <b>391</b> a second, however many writes there have been.</li>
    <li>Set <b>Bloom filters</b> to <b>One per file</b>. A filter says which files might hold the row, so a read opens only about <b>1.04</b> files, pulls <b>66.6</b> KB and a server does about <b>1,127</b> reads a second. That is close to the roughly 1200 the paper measured for random reads.</li>
    <li>Set <b>Block size</b> to <b>Small blocks</b>. Each read now pulls <b>8.3</b> KB instead of 66.6, and the same file system bandwidth serves about <b>9,014</b> reads a second.</li>
    <li>Finally set <b>Row looked up</b> to <b>A row that does not exist</b>. With filters, a lookup opens only <b>0.05</b> files, on average, so almost none reach the disk and the server is limited by its CPU, at <b>10,811</b> reads a second, the rate the paper measured for reads served from memory.</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="pg bgr" data-bgr>
    <div class="pg-row">
      <label>Writes so far <select class="pg-in" data-writes><option value="5000">Few writes</option><option value="100000">Many writes</option></select></label>
      <label>Compactions <select class="pg-in" data-compactions><option value="off">Never run</option><option value="on">Merge in the background</option></select></label>
      <label>Bloom filters <select class="pg-in" data-bloom><option value="off">Not used</option><option value="on">One per file</option></select></label>
      <label>Block size <select class="pg-in" data-block><option value="64">Default blocks</option><option value="8">Small blocks</option></select></label>
      <label>Row looked up <select class="pg-in" data-exists><option value="on">A row that exists</option><option value="off">A row that does not exist</option></select></label>
      <button type="button" class="btn" data-reset>Reset</button>
    </div>
    <div class="pg-grid">
      <div class="pg-box" data-b1><h3>Files in the tablet</h3><span class="pg-mono" data-o1></span><span class="pg-note" data-n1></span></div>
      <div class="pg-box" data-b2><h3>Files a read opens</h3><span class="pg-mono" data-o2></span><span class="pg-note" data-n2></span></div>
      <div class="pg-box" data-b3><h3>Data pulled per read</h3><span class="pg-mono" data-o3></span><span class="pg-note" data-n3></span></div>
      <div class="pg-box" data-b4><h3>Reads a server can do each second</h3><span class="pg-mono" data-o4></span><span class="pg-note" data-n4></span></div>
    </div>
    <p class="pg-out" data-verdict aria-live="polite"></p>
    <p class="pg-note">Model, partly from the paper. From the paper: a random read of a 1000-byte value pulled a 64 KB block from the file system, so one tablet server did about 1200 random reads a second, at about 75 MB a second, and most applications that read that way reduce the block size, typically to 8 KB; random reads served from memory reached 10,811 a second per server, which is used here as the CPU limit. Invented for teaching: a memtable fills after 1,000 writes and becomes a file; merging compactions keep a tablet at 5 files or fewer; a Bloom filter wrongly lets 1% of lookups through; a row that exists is in one file and a read opens files from newest to oldest until it finds it, so half of them on average. Without filters a row that does not exist makes a read open every file. Real systems are more complicated than this.</p>
  </div>
</PlaygroundShell>
<script>
  import { query, formatWholeNumber } from '../../playground';
  import { simulate } from '../../models/bigtable-reads.ts';
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-bgr]').forEach((root) => {
    const q = query(root);
    const MANY_FILES = 20; // more files than this turns the box red
    const SLOW_READS_PER_SECOND = 1_000; // fewer reads a second than this turns the box red
    const DEFAULTS: Record<string, string> = { writes: '5000', compactions: 'off', bloom: 'off', block: '64', exists: 'on' };
    const select = (key: string) => q<HTMLSelectElement>(`[data-${key}]`);
    // Seeks print to one decimal (3.0, 50.5) unless a second one matters (1.04, 0.05). Kilobytes print to one decimal with separators.
    const seeksText = (n: number) => { const hundredths = Math.round(n * 100) / 100; return Number.isInteger(hundredths * 10) ? hundredths.toFixed(1) : hundredths.toFixed(2); };
    const kbText = (n: number) => (Math.round(n * 10) / 10).toLocaleString('en-US', { minimumFractionDigits: 1, maximumFractionDigits: 1 });

    function draw() {
      const result = simulate({
        writes: Number(select('writes').value), compactions: select('compactions').value === 'on', bloomFilters: select('bloom').value === 'on',
        blockKb: Number(select('block').value), rowExists: select('exists').value === 'on',
      });
      q('[data-o1]').textContent = String(result.sstables);
      q('[data-n1]').textContent = select('compactions').value === 'on' ? 'merging compactions keep the count down' : 'every full memtable adds a file';
      q('[data-o2]').textContent = seeksText(result.seeksPerRead);
      q('[data-n2]').textContent = select('bloom').value === 'on' ? 'a filter says which files might hold the row' : select('exists').value === 'on' ? 'about half of them, until the row is found' : 'all of them, to be sure the row is not there';
      q('[data-o3]').textContent = `${kbText(result.kbPerRead)} KB`;
      q('[data-n3]').textContent = `one block of ${select('block').value} KB for each file opened`;
      q('[data-o4]').textContent = formatWholeNumber(result.readsPerSecond);
      q('[data-n4]').textContent = result.readsPerSecond >= 10_000 ? 'limited by the CPU, not the disk' : 'limited by the file system bandwidth';
      const bad = [result.sstables > MANY_FILES, result.seeksPerRead > 10, result.kbPerRead > 1_000, result.readsPerSecond < SLOW_READS_PER_SECOND];
      ['b1', 'b2', 'b3', 'b4'].forEach((b, i) => q<HTMLElement>(`[data-${b}]`).style.setProperty('--c', bad[i] ? 'var(--bad)' : 'var(--good)'));

      const out = q('[data-verdict]'); const broken = bad.some(Boolean); out.classList.toggle('bad', broken);
      if (result.sstables > MANY_FILES) out.textContent = `${result.sstables} files pile up and a read opens ${seeksText(result.seeksPerRead)} of them, so the server manages only ${formatWholeNumber(result.readsPerSecond)} reads a second. Turn on merging compactions.`;
      else if (result.readsPerSecond < SLOW_READS_PER_SECOND) out.textContent = `A read opens ${seeksText(result.seeksPerRead)} files and pulls ${kbText(result.kbPerRead)} KB, so the server does only ${formatWholeNumber(result.readsPerSecond)} reads a second. Try Bloom filters and smaller blocks.`;
      else out.textContent = `A read opens ${seeksText(result.seeksPerRead)} files and pulls ${kbText(result.kbPerRead)} KB, which lets a server do ${formatWholeNumber(result.readsPerSecond)} reads a second.`;
    }
    root.addEventListener('input', draw);
    root.addEventListener('change', draw);
    q('[data-reset]').addEventListener('click', () => {
      Object.keys(DEFAULTS).forEach((k) => (select(k).value = DEFAULTS[k]));
      draw();
    });
    draw();
  });
</script>
